  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1105 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs of 11:30pm, showers are moving northeast through our far \neastern counties. Scattered showers will continue in the eastern\nplains through much of the overnight period. Rain should\ngenerally remain light, with no hazards expected.\n\nA surface low is forecast to develop to our southeast on Thursday, \nturning surface winds more northerly in the morning and easterly by \nthe afternoon. PWs will decrease slightly as the turning of winds no \nlonger allows Gulf moisture to stream into the area at lower levels. \nHowever, the more easterly winds will allow for upslope for much of \nthe afternoon and evening on Thursday. Showers and an isolated storm \nor two are expected to form over the higher terrain, starting in \nthe southern Front Range foothills and Palmer Divide in the mid \nafternoon. Rain chances will then move into northern mountains \nand foothills later in the evening and early overnight period as \nwinds turn more southeasterly. PWs will remain around 140% to 190%\nof normal and storm motions will be pretty slow (10 to 15kts), so\nsome localized heavy rainfall and flash flooding will be possible\nin the late afternoon and evening. \n\nOn Friday, a shortwave will approach the area providing some upper \nlevel lift and scattered showers and storms. PWs will remain high \n(around 150% to 175% of normal), but storm motions are forecast to\nbe faster than Thursday, leading to slightly lower flash flooding\nconcerns. Models have rain chances increasing in the higher \nterrain for the early afternoon, with the rain chances pushing \neast throughout the afternoon and early evening. \n\nThe shortwave will move off to the east early Saturday, with upper \nlevel ridging building on the backside of the shortwave for the \nweekend. At the surface, a boundary looks to move through early \nSaturday, turning winds more westerly. These winds will bring drier \nand warmer downsloping air into the urban corridor and plains, \ndropping PWs and increasing highs back above normal for the weekend. \nRain chances will decrease significantly behind this boundary and \nhighs will reach into the low to mid 80s for much of the plains. \n\nThings start to get more interesting for the start of next week as a \ntrough approaches the area. There is still a lot of uncertainty with \nthis system, as some models have the trough digging south, with a \nlow developing near the Four Corners region. While other models \nkeep the trough more north, before it digs further south to our \neast. The positioning and strength of the trough will have \nsignificant impacts on the strength and timing of a cold front, \nour precip chances, and temperatures for Monday through Wednesday.\nRight now, models are showing a decent cold front moving through \nthe area sometime late Monday through early Tuesday. The \npositioning of that trough will determine if we can pull those \ncolder temperatures aloft south behind the front. This has lead \nto a pretty significant spread in temperatures between ensemble \nmembers, with highs on Tuesday ranging anywhere from the low 50s \nto near 70 (25th to 75th percentiles) for the plains. The \ntemperatures will have impacts on the snow chances in the higher \nterrain as well. Areas above 10,000ft are currently sitting at a \n20% to 40% chance for at least an inch of snow from late Monday \nthrough late Tuesday. If we can get those colder temperatures and \nbetter lift in our area, we could see those chances increase as we\nget closer.",
